      {
        "review_id": 154172046,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r154172046-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Life (and Death) in the Raw",
        "text": "This place is South Asia's answer to Federico Fellini.... \nPashupati is sacred to Hindus, especially followers of Shiva. To walk its extensive grounds is to stroll through some of Hinduism's most sacred lore: \"Here's where Lord Shiva leapt across the river\"; \"Here's where a part of Devi's body fell.\" (Buddhism's too: Tilopa and Naropa allegedly meditated there, on the banks of the Bagmati.) It's been around for more than 1700 years. So visiting it is like a wal through human history. There are gangs of monkeys and rows of fake holy men, preening their moustaches and demanding huge fees for photos. The paths along the river are an open air funeral parlor, where families cremate their dead loved ones. But the forest and temple complexes up bove are a tranquil place where real holy men meditate and pilgrims wander in peace. It's a bewildering, fascinating place. Worth a visit. Know that it's life \"unfiltered\".",
